WEBVTT

NOTE SE201 Lesson 3: Parallel agents, one integration — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.037
Software engineering 201. Lesson three.

2
00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:07.030
Parallel agents, one integration.

3
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.524
Quick question. Three agents each build one part of a system.

4
00:00:11.524 --> 00:00:15.124
All three say, done. All three pass their own checks.

5
00:00:15.124 --> 00:00:17.298
Is the system done? Yes? Almost?

6
00:00:17.298 --> 00:00:20.152
Or does nobody know yet? Nobody knows yet.

7
00:00:20.152 --> 00:00:22.801
Each check proves one part works alone.

8
00:00:22.801 --> 00:00:25.790
Not one of them has seen the parts together.

9
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.719
Picture a team that splits one feature across three agents.

10
00:00:29.719 --> 00:00:33.107
One builds the screen. One builds the service behind it.

11
00:00:33.107 --> 00:00:38.370
One changes the database. Each part gets finished, and each part is correct on its own.

12
00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.786
Then the parts meet. The screen asks for a field the service never sends.

13
00:00:42.786 --> 00:00:45.145
Two of the agents edited the same file.

14
00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:49.440
And nobody finds out until the very end, when it costs the most to fix.

15
00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.896
Parallel work is only fast if it comes back together cleanly.

16
00:00:53.896 --> 00:00:55.921
So plan the integration first.

17
00:00:55.921 --> 00:00:57.540
Before any agent starts.

18
00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.318
Three things. First, a shared contract.

19
00:01:00.318 --> 00:01:07.043
Write down what crosses between the parts: the interfaces, the formats, the names, and who owns which files.

20
00:01:07.043 --> 00:01:10.032
At enterprise scale, that's an API, or a schema.

21
00:01:10.032 --> 00:01:13.955
It can be one page. But it has to exist before the work starts.

22
00:01:13.955 --> 00:01:18.750
And when it has to change, change it in one place, where every agent sees it.

23
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.987
Then every agent gets its own brief, its own finish line, and its own check.

24
00:01:23.987 --> 00:01:27.460
And part of that check is: does it match the contract?

25
00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.688
Second, one merge point. One place where the parts come together.

26
00:01:31.688 --> 00:01:35.566
So when something doesn't fit, there's exactly one place to look.

27
00:01:35.566 --> 00:01:39.265
And use it early. Join rough versions of the parts on day one.

28
00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.950
Don't let the first meeting be the last step.

29
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.109
Third, test the whole system.

30
00:01:44.109 --> 00:01:47.603
Not just each part. The assembled thing, running as one.

31
00:01:47.603 --> 00:01:50.660
That's the check no single agent could run alone.

32
00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:57.325
Here's a real one. Lessons three, four and five of software engineering 101 were built at the same time.

33
00:01:57.325 --> 00:02:00.421
Three scripts, three renders, running side by side.

34
00:02:00.421 --> 00:02:04.550
The three finished videos landed within forty seconds of each other.

35
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.500
And they came back together in one place.

36
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:14.223
The contract already existed: the same script format the first two lessons used, and the same page fields.

37
00:02:14.223 --> 00:02:19.296
One merge point: one content file, read by one generator that builds every page.

38
00:02:19.296 --> 00:02:21.897
And they shipped together, in one change.

39
00:02:21.897 --> 00:02:24.116
Today that path has two more gates.

40
00:02:24.116 --> 00:02:29.253
One check that walks the whole site: every link, and every file a page points to.

41
00:02:29.253 --> 00:02:32.361
And a pull request to staging, for me to approve.

42
00:02:32.361 --> 00:02:36.420
This lesson and the one before it were drafted in parallel, too.

43
00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.616
Parallel isn't free. You pay for it at the merge.

44
00:02:39.616 --> 00:02:44.958
With the integration planned first, three agents finish sooner than one working down a list.

45
00:02:44.958 --> 00:02:48.211
Without a plan, the time you saved comes back as rework.

46
00:02:48.211 --> 00:02:52.740
And if two parts keep needing each other's answers, they aren't parallel work.

47
00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:53.960
Go back to the graph.

48
00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:57.532
So, to recap. Split only where nothing crosses.

49
00:02:57.532 --> 00:03:00.275
Agree the contract before anyone starts.

50
00:03:00.275 --> 00:03:04.800
Merge early, in one place. And test the whole, not just the parts.

51
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.880
Which gives us rule three, for your agents.

52
00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.499
Before you run agents in parallel, plan how the work comes back together.

53
00:03:12.499 --> 00:03:14.523
Write the shared contract first.

54
00:03:14.523 --> 00:03:18.952
Give each agent its own brief, its own finish line, and its own check.

55
00:03:18.952 --> 00:03:23.570
Merge early, in one place. And test the whole system, not just each part.

56
00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.297
Now, your turn. Think of the last time work was split, across agents, or across people.

57
00:03:29.297 --> 00:03:31.212
Where did the parts first meet?

58
00:03:31.212 --> 00:03:33.190
And who checked the whole thing?

59
00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.298
At that merge point, someone still has to say yes.

60
00:03:37.298 --> 00:03:40.681
That's next. Governance: where people decide.

61
00:03:40.681 --> 00:03:42.410
See you in lesson four.
